(*
expect:
true
true
false
true
true
done
*)
(* rete#184: the two "arm a callback" seams pin the message oop in an M6 handle,
   and neither used to give the previous one back. The handle table is 32 slots
   (handles.h) and handle_new ABORTS the VM when it fills, so on the ship's bell —
   one re-arm per half-hourly strike — slot 32 was taken ~15.5 h after boot: a hard
   uptime ceiling by construction. Confirmed in the field by 3431 sys/metrics
   samples (#153): handlesLive stepped +1 every thirty minutes, on the half hour,
   with handlesLive == handlesHighWater in every sample.

   Both leak paths are reachable HOST-side, which is what makes this a suite test
   rather than a board observation:

     - rtcAlarmAt:post:on: — the host has no DS3231 and REFUSES every arm (the host
       rtc_alarm_arm answers -1 on purpose, so a node has to carry its fallback), so
       the host exercises the FAILURE path, where the pin was leaked before it had
       even been handed to a driver.
     - sensorArm:post:on:threshold: — the host backend arms 'accel' successfully, so the
       host exercises the RE-ARM path, which is the one the bell walks.

   Every assertion is a DELTA, never an absolute. What is wrong with a leak is that
   the count grows with the number of arms, so the property is that 20 arms cost
   exactly what 1 arm costs; an absolute would encode whatever else this suite
   happens to have pinned and would go red for the wrong reason. *)
class ArmSeamsHandleTest packageUsing: manifest = () (
public main: platform args: args = (
  | base afterRefusals afterFirst afterMany |
  base:: (platform metrics) at: #hnl ifAbsent: [ -1 ].

  (* The refusal path. It answers nil, and it must cost nothing. *)
  ((platform rtcAlarmAt: 3600 post: #alarm on: 'do/bell') == nil) printString println.
  1 to: 20 do: [ :i | platform rtcAlarmAt: 3600 post: #alarm on: 'do/bell' ].
  afterRefusals:: (platform metrics) at: #hnl ifAbsent: [ -1 ].
  (afterRefusals = base) printString println.

  (* The re-arm path. There is exactly one outstanding trigger per node, so an
     armed seam costs exactly one pin however many times it is re-armed. *)
  ((platform sensorArm: 'accel' post: #moved on: 'obs/accel' threshold: 1000) == nil) printString println.
  afterFirst:: (platform metrics) at: #hnl ifAbsent: [ -1 ].
  (afterFirst = (base + 1)) printString println.
  1 to: 20 do: [ :i | platform sensorArm: 'accel' post: #moved on: 'obs/accel' threshold: 1000 ].
  afterMany:: (platform metrics) at: #hnl ifAbsent: [ -1 ].
  (afterMany = afterFirst) printString println.
  'done' println.
)
) : (
)
